# Gizmo handles and interaction architecture implementation plan

Source proposal: [GizmoHandles.proposal.md](./GizmoHandles.proposal.md)

Status: Execution in progress. Phases 1 through 3 were implemented and independently audited on 2026-10-27; later phases remain unchecked. The foundation is inactive in gameplay. Leave tasks unchecked until their stated verification gates pass.

## Goal and scope

Refactor the existing positioning controls into persistent owned GizmoHandle instances with nullable state-based appearance and seven independently replaceable interaction boundaries:

1. GizmoDragInputBehavior and IGizmoDragTarget: gesture lifecycle through existing global routing/capture.
2. IGizmoHitTarget: handle identification, distance, priority and state feedback.
3. IGizmoFrameSource: logical origin, axis and plane basis.
4. Axis/plane interaction targets: pointer-to-parameter measurement.
5. IInteractionParameterSource<TParameter>: parameter editing, interpretation and constraints.
6. IGizmoValueSource<TValue>: access through the existing notifying Value setter.
7. IGizmoEditStateTarget: existing baseline and application transaction coordination.

Preserve current endpoint/midpoint behavior, rendering, labels, snapping, precision and rollback. Handle IDs identify controls; they must not imply shape, styling or operation.

Non-goals: new production rotation/scaling tools, new shape catalogs, design-mode features, another undo stack, another authoritative value store, independent input/render subscriptions, terrain-dependent dragging, persistence-format changes, legacy-config migration, HUD redesign, resize-policy changes or wireframe/cloud occlusion fixes.

## Assumptions, decisions and evidence rules

- The source proposal is authoritative. Earlier gizmo plans provide historical context, not permission to restore superseded behavior.
- Preserve the exact names readonly record struct AppearenceProperties and EAppearenceState. Other proposed contract names may be refined coherently, but none of the seven boundaries may be collapsed away.
- Before implementing consumers, record the exact nullable visual fields, units, validation rules, shape/label-required baseline fields and invalidation categories. These are implementation details to derive from existing visuals, not authorization for new visual features.
- Specify initialization/unwind ordering, adapter ownership and disposal, rejection reporting and reentrant callbacks in Phase 1. Do not treat unresolved lifecycle details as silent defaults.
- Reuse existing manager, typed Value setter, constraint delegates, transaction notifications and renderer resources. Adapt them to contracts rather than adding parallel systems.
- Foundation phases remain inactive in normal gameplay until Phase 6. Retaining the existing production path during construction is temporary, not a feature flag or compatibility mode; remove it after verified cutover.
- Every phase includes Doxygen-compatible XML documentation for new/changed contracts and non-obvious lifecycle details, including parameters, returns and validation exceptions.
- Record concise task -> proposal requirement -> source/test evidence as implementation proceeds. Separate source review, automated tests, build/JSON results and actual in-game evidence.
- Missing live access/results leave the corresponding tasks blocked and unchecked. Passing mocks or a build does not prove rendered output.

## Phase 1: Define contracts and qualify existing lifecycle integration

Prerequisites: None.
Outcome: Each boundary has a documented, testable contract and a single integration path into current systems.

    ✔ Read the complete proposal and trace its linked implementation before adding abstractions.
        ✔ Trace Gizmo, Gizmo<TValue>, manager/input routing, PositionGizmo, geometry/render adapters and WorkAreaGizmoSession.
        ✔ Identify current baseline restoration, value events, owner preview/commit/cancel notifications, capture suppression and error-reporting paths.
        ✔ Record existing visual dimensions, pick tolerances, solver thresholds and resource ownership to preserve.
    ✔ Define the seven replaceable contracts and typed/delegate adapters needed by current positioning.
        ✔ Separate gesture routing, hit testing, spatial frames, measurement, parameter modification, value access and edit-state coordination.
        ✔ Define hit results containing owned handle identity, finite valid distance and deterministic priority/tie ordering.
        ✔ Define scalar and two-dimensional parameter contracts without requiring all values to be transforms.
        ✔ Keep typed parameter/value implementations behind non-generic input lifecycle; avoid boxing, runtime value casts and silent setters.
    ✔ Specify lifecycle and failure contracts before their consumers.
        ✔ Validate/initialize measurement before accepted begin; initialize parameter modification and edit-state scope before successful-start notification.
        ✔ Specify unwind for each partially initialized scope, explicit rejection/error reporting and exactly-once cleanup.
        ✔ Specify end-modification as bookkeeping only, normal completion as commit and forced termination as cancellation.
        ✔ Preserve notifying baseline restoration before cancellation notification; avoid duplicate owner notifications or rollback.
        ✔ Define capture-local versus shared configuration ownership and reentrancy rules.
    ✔ Add contract-level tests/fakes demonstrating independent replacement of the seven boundaries.

Completion criteria:

    ✔ Contracts compile and focused tests establish lifecycle ordering and boundary independence without activating a new gameplay path.
    ✔ Every existing input, value and transaction responsibility has one documented owner; no duplicated capture, rollback or persistence owner is required.

Verification and traceability (2026-10-27):

- Source qualification -> proposal [positioning compatibility baseline](GizmoHandles.proposal.md#positioning-compatibility-baseline), [appearance fields and validation](GizmoHandles.proposal.md#appearance-fields-and-validation), and [edit-state targets](GizmoHandles.proposal.md#edit-state-targets), recording preservation requirements and integration decisions. [Architecture documentation](BuildBrushArchitecture.md#reusable-interaction-contracts) describes the implemented contract APIs, not execution history. Inherited input/precision/cancellation requirements were consulted at immutable revision `04172078d76652ec9fc5610351b926c66daf0399`; the retired proposal reference identifies that revision.
- Seven boundaries and typed access -> proposal Required architectural boundaries -> [GizmoInteractionContracts.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/Gizmos/GizmoInteractionContracts.cs), including the real notifying value adapter. IGizmoHandle defines identity/ownership for the later concrete class, not a second wrapper.
- Owned hit/frame contracts -> proposal Geometry snapshots and Spatial-frame sources -> [GizmoHandleHit.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/Gizmos/GizmoHandleHit.cs) and [GizmoFrame.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/Gizmos/GizmoFrame.cs), with validation, local tie ordering and current-dimension checks.
- Lifecycle/replacement evidence -> proposal Edit-state targets and Capture, cancellation and mutation -> [GizmoInteractionContractTests.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests/Gizmos/GizmoInteractionContractTests.cs), using independent fakes and the real notifying value source. Production measurement, input and transaction adapters remain later-phase work.
- Delegated Release/net10.0 regression command: `dotnet test .\VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests\VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests.csproj -c Release --framework net10.0 --filter "FullyQualifiedName~Gizmos|FullyQualifiedName~WorkAreaGizmoSession"` with `VINTAGE_STORY=G:\Vintagestory`: 277 passed, 0 failed/skipped. This includes 20 new contract cases.
- After fixing new test nullable-flow warnings, the delegated `FullyQualifiedName~GizmoInteractionContractTests` rerun passed 20/20 with no new-source/test warnings. Existing unrelated nullable/analyzer and package-advisory warnings remain.
- Delegated normal `.\build.ps1 --target=Build` and ValidateJson passed; whitespace checks passed. No packaging or live-game qualification was claimed.
- Second implementation review completed; the fresh read-only audit-stage-completion audit independently consulted governing documents and sources and concluded Phase 1 fully satisfied with no required issues or evidence gaps. Only this phase is checked complete.

## Phase 2: Implement persistent handle ownership and appearance resolution

Prerequisites: Phase 1.
Outcome: Handles are stable owned objects with validated, independently resolved appearance properties.

    ✔ Implement GizmoHandle configuration and controlled owner collection operations.
        ✔ Include stable ID, owner, local visibility/enabled state, frame/hit/interaction configuration and optional label.
        ✔ Expose a read-only collection; reject duplicate IDs, double attachment and attachment to another owner.
        ✔ Require detach before transfer; clear ownership and subscriptions on removal/disposal.
        ✔ Establish actual-handle hover/capture references for the new path, retaining IDs only where useful for events/diagnostics.
    ✔ Implement readonly record struct AppearenceProperties and EAppearenceState.
        ✔ Make every appearance property nullable; include shape, size, Vector4 RGBA colors (including opacity), line width and configurable label styling.
        ✔ Encapsulate Dictionary<EAppearenceState, AppearenceProperties>; expose read-only configuration views and atomic validated mutation operations.
        ✔ Require Default throughout the attached lifetime with all fields required by the configured shape/label.
        ✔ Resolve each field as activeOverride.Field ?? defaultEntry.Field; absent state entries behave as all-null overrides.
        ✔ Resolve Selected directly against Default, never through Hover; Selected means captured and takes precedence over Hover.
        ✔ Reject incomplete/invalid configurations before publishing them; produce fully validated resolved snapshots without renderer fallback values.
        ✔ Distinguish spatial invalidation from color/style changes and ordinary state transitions.
    ✔ Test ownership, eligibility and every nullable appearance field.
        ✔ Cover duplicate IDs, double attachment, transfer, detachment and idempotent disposal.
        ✔ Cover missing overrides, mixed-field fallback, baseline removal/incompleteness, invalid values and unchanged configuration on rejection.
        ✔ Cover state precedence and mutations during hover/capture without making hidden/ineligible handles interactive.

Completion criteria:

    ✔ Focused tests prove per-field resolution and ownership invariants; production positioning remains unchanged.
    ✔ Configuration cannot bypass validation/invalidation through exposed mutable collections.

Verification and traceability (2026-10-27):

- Ownership/configuration -> proposal [ownership and component model](GizmoHandles.proposal.md#ownership-and-component-model) and [capture, cancellation and mutation](GizmoHandles.proposal.md#capture-cancellation-and-mutation) -> [GizmoHandle.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/Gizmos/GizmoHandle.cs), [Gizmo.Handles.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/Gizmos/Gizmo.Handles.cs) and [Gizmo.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/Gizmos/Gizmo.cs): controlled membership, actual-instance state, eligibility, cancellation and reentrant cleanup. Removing the last handle does not restore ID-only interaction semantics.
- Appearance -> proposal [appearance contract](GizmoHandles.proposal.md#appearance-contract) and [appearance fields and validation](GizmoHandles.proposal.md#appearance-fields-and-validation) -> [AppearenceProperties.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/Gizmos/AppearenceProperties.cs): ten nullable fields, including three whole RGBA System.Numerics.Vector4 colors, direct Default fallback, complete validated snapshots and differentiated invalidation.
- [GizmoHandleTests.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests/Gizmos/GizmoHandleTests.cs) covers every nullable field, protected maps, rejected mutations, ownership/transfer/disposal, actual-state precedence and capture mutations. Added regression coverage ensures throwing state observers cannot skip rollback and reentrant disposal defers resources until termination finishes.
- Final delegated Release/net10.0 regression command: `dotnet test .\VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests\VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests.csproj -c Release --framework net10.0 --filter "FullyQualifiedName~Gizmos|FullyQualifiedName~WorkAreaGizmoSession"` with `VINTAGE_STORY=G:\Vintagestory`: 302 passed, 0 failed/skipped. Normal Build, ValidateJson and `git diff --check` passed; no new-file compiler/analyzer warnings. Existing seven production nullable warnings and dependency advisories remain.
- Second implementation review and independent read-only completion audit passed after resolving and independently rechecking the lifecycle finding. Production PositionGizmo remains on its existing path; no live graphics result or later-phase completion is claimed.

## Phase 3: Implement spatial sources, shared geometry and replaceable hit targets

Prerequisites: Phases 1 and 2.
Outcome: Rendering and picking can consume persistent handles without supplying movement semantics.

    ✔ Implement position/axis and camera-derived IGizmoFrameSource adapters.
        ✔ Supply validated origin, direction, dimension and orthogonal plane tangents where required.
        ✔ Keep logical frames independent of meshes and numeric IDs; support capture-time frame snapshots.
    ✔ Derive immutable per-frame geometry referencing its owning handle.
        ✔ Replace the generated-part assumption with resolved shape, transform, dimensions and camera/value/appearance context.
        ✔ Use the same frame and resolved appearance for visual geometry and default picking.
        ✔ Preserve screen-sized controls, distant-coordinate precision, overlay priority and existing minimum hit tolerances.
        ✔ Rebuild affected geometry for shape/size/frame changes; avoid spatial rebuilds for color-only changes.
    ✔ Implement geometry-backed IGizmoHitTarget and aggregate/arbitrary-handle dispatch.
        ✔ Return only valid owned handles with distance/priority and deterministic ties.
        ✔ Route hover/interacting feedback to existing handle state rather than a second state store.
        ✔ Allow hit eligibility to be disabled without hiding the shape.
        ✔ Support deliberate custom pick padding/geometry without changing measurement behavior.
    ✔ Test frame validation and frame use without meshes.
    ✔ Test default draw/hit snapshot agreement, state-dependent shape/size changes and custom hit-target replacement.
    ✔ Test arbitrary handle IDs and aggregate dispatch without predefined transform-part identifiers.

Completion criteria:

    ✔ Geometry/hit/frame tests pass independently of pointer measurement and application values.
    ✔ Rendering and default picking agree on resolved geometry; intentional custom hit geometry is explicit and tested.

Verification and traceability (2026-10-27):

- Logical frames -> proposal [spatial-frame sources and measurement targets](GizmoHandles.proposal.md#spatial-frame-sources-and-measurement-targets) -> [GizmoFrameSources.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/Gizmos/GizmoFrameSources.cs): live position/axis, camera-facing plane and fixed-frame adapters, validated independently of meshes and IDs. Captured frame values remain immutable when live position/camera readers change.
- Shared geometry -> proposal [geometry snapshots](GizmoHandles.proposal.md#geometry-snapshots), [positioning compatibility baseline](GizmoHandles.proposal.md#positioning-compatibility-baseline) and [appearance fields and validation](GizmoHandles.proposal.md#appearance-fields-and-validation) -> [GizmoHandleGeometry.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/Gizmos/GizmoHandleGeometry.cs): immutable owner/handle/context/frame geometry plus complete appearance snapshots; cached spatial data survives color/style-only changes. Clipping, screen-size clamps, state-dependent geometry and label anchors use shared primitive transforms. [GizmoScreenPicking.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/Gizmos/GizmoScreenPicking.cs) shares the unchanged screen-distance math with legacy positioning.
- Picking/feedback -> proposal [input behavior and hit targets](GizmoHandles.proposal.md#input-behavior-and-hit-targets) and [capture, cancellation and mutation](GizmoHandles.proposal.md#capture-cancellation-and-mutation) -> [GizmoHitTargets.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/Gizmos/GizmoHitTargets.cs): geometry-backed last-published-context picking, explicit custom padding/delegate picking, immutable aggregates, actual-member validation and deterministic deduplicated ranks. Final post-callback checks reject stale ownership and filter changed eligibility.
- [GizmoGeometryTests.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests/Gizmos/GizmoGeometryTests.cs) adds 24 cases covering frames without meshes, frozen bases, all primitive shapes, tilted frames, matching legacy vertices within absolute `1e-10` per component, draw/pick parity at GUI scales/clamps and large coordinates, spatial cache reuse, clipping, disabled-but-visible shapes, custom picks, arbitrary IDs and reentrant callbacks. The vertex tolerance accommodates legacy billboard unprojection roundoff rather than weakening spatial behavior.
- Final delegated Release/net10.0 regression command: `dotnet test .\VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests\VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests.csproj -c Release --framework net10.0 --filter "FullyQualifiedName~Gizmos|FullyQualifiedName~WorkAreaGizmoSession"` with `VINTAGE_STORY=G:\Vintagestory`: 326 passed, 0 failed/skipped. Normal Build, ValidateJson and `git diff --check` passed; no changed/new gizmo compiler warnings. Eight existing nullable production diagnostics and existing dependency advisories were observed.
- Second implementation review and independent read-only completion audit passed. The audit's missing XML null-exception documentation was corrected and independently rechecked; no executable code changed after successful verification. Native routing, production renderer/label consumption and actual graphics qualification remain later-phase work.

## Phase 4: Extract measurement, parameter, value and edit-state implementations

Prerequisites: Phases 1 and 3.
Outcome: Existing analytic movement works through the new contracts without knowing shapes, IDs or application persistence.

    ☐ Extract axis and camera-facing-plane solvers into IGizmoDragTarget implementations with capture-local sessions.
        ☐ Preserve drag-start frame, initial grab offset, initial/last accepted measurements and existing numerical thresholds.
        ☐ Retain degeneracy, excessive-jump, nonfinite/out-of-range coordinate and dimension rejection.
        ☐ Freeze the center plane at begin; camera movement must not rebase capture.
        ☐ Reject invalid measurements explicitly using current notice/error paths; do not introduce terrain fallback.
    ☐ Implement typed position parameter sources and IGizmoValueSource<TValue> adapter.
        ☐ Provide read/begin/set/end-modification lifecycle for scalar and planar parameters.
        ☐ Record the initial parameter/value relationship and map deltas to candidates through existing constraints/snapping delegates.
        ☐ Advance last-accepted measurements only as intended after constraint acceptance.
        ☐ Delegate reads/writes to Gizmo<TValue>.Value with existing equality/no-op notification behavior.
        ☐ Preserve immutable typed values, no silent assignments, no secondary authoritative storage and no assumption that all values are transforms.
    ☐ Implement IGizmoEditStateTarget over existing baseline and owner transaction paths.
        ☐ Coordinate accepted begin, preview, completion and cancellation without adding undo/persistence infrastructure.
        ☐ End parameter modification without independently committing; unwind all initialized scopes on failed begin.
        ☐ Restore once through the notifying setter and invoke existing owner cancellation once before the canceled notification.
    ☐ Test measurement independently from application values and parameter adapters independently from pointer input.
    ☐ Test notifying value access, constraint rejection, no-op owner echoes and transaction coordination.
    ☐ Demonstrate the same scalar measurement with two different typed parameter adapters using test-only consumers.
        ☐ Demonstrate replaceable frame/hit sources without changing solver code or adding role dispatch.
        ☐ Do not turn the test demonstration into new production scaling/rotation tools.

Completion criteria:

    ☐ Analytic and composition tests pass with current precision, constraint and notification behavior.
    ☐ Lifecycle tests prove partial-begin unwind, end-modification without commit, forced-termination cancellation and no duplicate restoration/owner notifications.

## Phase 5: Compose handles with manager-owned gesture routing

Prerequisites: Phases 2, 3 and 4.
Outcome: The complete new input/capture path is integration-tested but not yet substituted for gameplay.

    ☐ Implement GizmoDragInputBehavior through existing manager/input adapter routing.
        ☐ Keep global hit arbitration and exclusive capture in the manager; do not subscribe per handle or run competing adapters.
        ☐ Route eligibility/press/drag/release/termination to the selected target without gesture code implementing solver math.
        ☐ Preserve free-cursor/reticle operation, GUI/handled-input priority and existing brush-action suppression.
    ☐ Compose existing Center/X/Y/Z controls using explicit handles, frame/hit/measurement/parameter/value/edit-state contracts.
        ☐ Preserve endpoint circle/diamond markers, RGB axis colors, opacity 0.8/1.0 and line widths 1.6/2.8.
        ☐ Ensure IDs are identity/tie-break information, not generic shape, color or operation selectors.
    ☐ Wire mutation and lifecycle cancellation through the single capture path.
        ☐ Cancel before removing/hiding/disabling a captured handle or making its owner ineligible, dimension-changed, removed or disposed.
        ☐ Cancel before replacing captured interaction/hit/frame/parameter/value/edit-state configuration.
        ☐ Preserve cancel-then-assign for genuinely different external authoritative values.
        ☐ Do not cancel for ordinary hover/selected transitions, appearance edits or label-only changes; preserve solver baselines.
        ☐ Release sessions, callbacks and capture exactly once, including reentrant completion/cancellation/disposal.
    ☐ Integration-test global exclusivity, rejected begin, notification ordering and capture mutation through the actual manager.
    ☐ Verify alternate IDs and replacement contracts preserve configured behavior without central dispatch edits.

Completion criteria:

    ☐ New-path manager integration tests pass, including repeated edits and lifecycle cancellation.
    ☐ Existing gameplay path remains the only active path until label/render/work-area integration is verified in Phase 6.

## Phase 6: Migrate labels/rendering and cut over work-area integration

Prerequisites: Phase 5.
Outcome: Production positioning uses the new architecture with full visual, label and transaction parity.

    ☐ Move label ownership to GizmoHandle and update predicate/context access as needed.
        ☐ Preserve Always/Hover/Selected/Predicate semantics; Hover remains distinct from capture.
        ☐ Evaluate predicates only after owner/handle eligibility checks.
        ☐ Preserve framebuffer-pixel offsets, top-left placement, positive right/down, arrow-tip anchors and center-origin anchors.
        ☐ Keep white text, padding and black background at 65% opacity as defaults.
    ☐ Update GizmoRenderAdapter and label rendering to consume resolved handle configuration.
        ☐ Remove ID-dependent styling; update anchors after state-dependent geometry changes.
        ☐ Keep native textures/shaders out of handles and retain renderer-owned GPU/state restoration and disposal.
        ☐ Key caches by persistent handle identity within the owner; refresh only affected text/scale/style resources.
        ☐ Prune detached/disposed handles and removed labels; verify shader/resource failure cleanup and no leaks.
    ☐ Update WorkAreaGizmoSession compositions and existing callers together.
        ☐ Keep endpoint center text localized Center and midpoint center text synchronized with selected-area name, rename and locale updates.
        ☐ Retain axis labels and current visibility defaults; label-only changes must not affect capture or hit geometry.
        ☐ Preserve endpoint and whole-area midpoint constraints, dimension checks, grid snapping and existing preview/commit/cancel wiring.
        ☐ Confirm drag previews do not write config; completion/cancellation keep existing persistence semantics.
        ☐ Keep persistence format, HUD controls, resize policy, creation-only terrain targeting and wireframe rendering unchanged.
    ☐ Run focused label/render/work-area/manager regressions before cutover.
    ☐ Switch the existing composition path to the new architecture without a second input/render registration.
        ☐ Remove temporary production-path bridging after passing the focused gate; retain no legacy interaction mode.
        ☐ Verify repeated endpoint and midpoint interactions through the actual manager/session/controller path.

Completion criteria:

    ☐ Relevant automated tests demonstrate behavior/resource parity and a single active production path.
    ☐ Labels, rendering and work-area transactions use handles and all seven contracts; no persisted data migration is introduced.

## Phase 7: Remove superseded code, document and qualify delivery

Prerequisites: Phase 6.
Outcome: The refactor is coherent, documented and qualified with separate automated/build/live evidence.

    ☐ Remove obsolete numeric-ID styling/operation switches, separate label map, generated-part assumptions, solver fields and duplicate helpers.
        ☐ Keep named ID constants only as optional identity conveniences.
        ☐ Remove construction scaffolding/bridges and obsolete tests; replace assertions with tests of the new contracts.
        ☐ Confirm no new global subscriptions, undo/value stores, terrain-drag path or silent error fallbacks remain.
    ☐ Update BuildBrushArchitecture.md and directly related public API documentation for the implemented ownership and seven boundaries.
        ☐ Document handle composition, property fallback, custom hit targets, parameter/value adapters and lifecycle ordering.
        ☐ Verify Doxygen-compatible XML docs for all new/changed contracts and relevant lifecycle details.
        ☐ Keep documents project-neutral and distinguish proposal/history from actual implementation.
    ☐ Run focused current tests, then the combined affected integration regression set.
        ☐ Cover ownership, every nullable field, snapshot consistency, arbitrary IDs, all seven contracts, composition and reentrant lifecycle.
        ☐ Cover analytic axis/plane behavior, large coordinates, dimension checks, work-area snapping and preview persistence boundaries.
        ☐ Cover all label modes, placement, rename/localization, style refresh and resource pruning.
        ☐ Record command, results and scope; investigate tightly coupled failures without fixing unrelated issues.
    ☐ Run the repository's normal Build and JSON validation; record warnings/failures separately from test results.
        ☐ Use the normal Build target, not a packaging/cleanup target; no new package deliverable is requested.
        ☐ Check whitespace and scope of final changes without modifying unrelated work.
    ☐ Perform actual in-game qualification and record tested version/settings and observations.
        ☐ Verify RGB arrows, circle/diamond centers, opacity/line-width feedback and screen-sized controls at relevant camera distances.
        ☐ Verify overlapping-handle selection and deterministic capture through free cursor and mouse-look reticle.
        ☐ Verify repeated endpoint/midpoint movement, fixed camera-plane behavior, grid snapping and unchanged area dimensions during whole-area translation.
        ☐ Verify completion, cancellation, focus/dimension/lifecycle invalidation and no brush placement leakage.
        ☐ Verify label backgrounds, offsets/anchors, hover visibility, rename updates and cleanup on area/session removal.
        ☐ Exercise large-coordinate rendering/picking and distinguish genuine visual evidence from mocked projection tests.

Completion criteria:

    ☐ No superseded production path or duplicated ownership remains; documentation reflects the implementation.
    ☐ Relevant tests, normal build, JSON validation and whitespace checks pass with recorded evidence.
    ☐ Required in-game checks pass, or remain explicitly blocked/unchecked; automated results are not substituted.

## Proposal coverage map

- Purpose/scope and required architectural boundaries -> Phases 1, 4 and 5; non-goals above.
- Persistent handle ownership and identity -> Phases 2 and 5.
- Nullable appearance, baseline validation and state resolution -> Phase 2; visual consumption in Phases 3 and 6.
- Geometry snapshots, spatial frames and replaceable hit targets -> Phases 3 and 5.
- Measurement, parameter sources, notifying value access and edit-state targets -> Phases 1 and 4.
- Capture/cancellation/mutation and exactly-once cleanup -> Phases 1, 4 and 5.
- Label semantics, renderer resources and live work-area naming -> Phase 6.
- Migration, compatibility removal and Doxygen documentation -> Phases 1-7, with cutover in Phase 6 and cleanup in Phase 7.
- Independent contract/composition tests and behavioral regression -> Each implementing phase plus Phase 7.
- Normal build/JSON and actual live rendering qualification -> Phase 7.

## Final acceptance

    ☐ Every proposal requirement has implementation and evidence; any deviation requires explicit approval rather than silent deferral.
    ☐ All seven responsibility boundaries are independently replaceable and exercised by tests.
    ☐ Existing work-area interaction, labels, precision, rendering/resource lifecycle and transaction behavior are preserved.
    ☐ No out-of-scope feature, legacy path, persisted format change or duplicated ownership was added.
    ☐ Required documentation, cleanup, automated checks and live-runtime evidence are complete.
    ☐ Only verified work is checked off; existing unrelated project tasks and historical completion records remain unchanged.
